WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.349 --> 00:00:06.709
The pound has dropped dramatically in the
months since the Brexit vote, but what does

00:00:06.709 --> 00:00:07.709
this really mean?

00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:08.709
Who dropped it?

00:00:08.709 --> 00:00:10.340
Is it down the back of the sofa?

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:15.790
And if you dropped it but I find it, can I
spend it on a new suit, because I have already

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:17.250
done that I’m afraid.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.560
Many of us have had that moment on holiday
where you stare at the bar bill and wonder

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:26.150
“does this mean I have to sell a kidney
or that I can now afford to pickle them in

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:27.150
champagne?”

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.710
How much horse cheese can I get for 5000 tögrög?

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:34.379
If I see a Malaysian bell, how much should
I pay to ringgit

00:00:34.379 --> 00:00:39.640
And if these jeans cost a million dong, will
I ever stop finding that funny?

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:48.540
What we want to find out today is; how much
is my money really worth?

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.360
Currency has existed in some form for over
ten thousand years.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:58.030
Livestock, such as cows or camels, were used
for millennia as a kind of standard when it

00:00:58.030 --> 00:00:59.030
came to bartering.

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:05.420
Cowry shells are probably the oldest currency
where the thing itself is not inherently useful,

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:10.759
I mean, we all love a good shiny shell but
they are a little too crunchy for dinner and

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:14.130
not much use in a battle.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:19.960
The logic of shells as money is the same as
metal coins as they are durable, they don’t

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:21.770
decay and they are not easy to fake.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:27.579
But it wasn’t until metal coins appeared
that currency trading really began.

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:32.610
The Chinese were streets ahead of everyone
else since they developed coins, and later

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.829
paper money, much earlier than it became common
elsewhere.

00:01:35.829 --> 00:01:41.329
But it would have been around the Mediterranean
and the so called Holy Land that changes to

00:01:41.329 --> 00:01:44.430
the monetary system really started to take
off.

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:49.490
Most of the time valuing currencies was a
simple matter of weight since coins were of

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:54.850
different sizes and contained different amounts
of precious metals, so if a Greek coin contained

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:59.400
more gold than an Egyptian one, it would be
assigned a higher value.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.689
This association with precious metal continued
and many currencies in the 19th century had

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:11.410
a gold or silver standard, where their currency
was set to a specific value of that metal,

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:15.130
which enabled a simple way to trade reliably
between nations.

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:22.300
The Scandinavian Monetary Union, for example,
began in 1873 between Denmark and Sweden,

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.390
with Norway joining two years later, when
they realised they could get a much better

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.770
deal on pickled herring if they joined.

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:34.580
The Swedes changed their currency to the Krona,
or crown as it means, and made each worth

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:38.300
0.403 grams of gold.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:43.790
The Danish rigsdaler became worth half a crown
and the Norwegian speciedaler was a quarter

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:44.790
crown.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:48.970
But in the 20th century, the gold standard
was eventually abandoned by most nations,

00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:54.330
thanks to the two world wars, and most currency
we have now is known as “fiat money”,

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:59.290
meaning that it’s only value is that people
believe it has value, rather than representing

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:05.640
some quantity of gold, silver, rice, cows
or the hatred of your entire family.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.680
Of course, your currency doesn’t just change
in relation to other currencies; it changes

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:17.190
value in relation to itself, in what we call
inflation or deflation.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:23.360
Fifty years ago, in the US, the average house
price was about $14,000 whereas now it’s

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.100
up around $200,000.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:30.400
This isn’t down to any great improvement
in the quality of the housing; sure you might

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.760
have double-glazed windows and a fridge that
dispenses emoticon shaped ice cubes, but your

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:40.090
grandad had a blanket and drank his whiskey
from an old bean can, so who do you think

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:41.090
is cooler?

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:44.650
Inflation is where a currency, let’s say
a dollar, can’t buy the same amount as it

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:46.540
could before.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:50.590
Most economies tend to experience inflation
for a number of reasons.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:54.890
Firstly, if business costs go up, they will
pass this on to customers.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:58.690
Say there’s a shortage of oil because of
an accident or a war.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:03.650
The demand for oil is still the same but now
there’s less available, so oil is more valuable

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:08.490
and you’ll need more money to buy it.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:13.030
If you’re a company who uses a lot of oil,
now you’re spending more money, so, to cover

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:16.489
this loss, you make your product more expensive.

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:21.510
Another way might be if a government lowers
taxes, then people have more money to spend.

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:26.760
But businesses realise this, so they just
raise their prices to make more money themselves.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:31.480
Governments try to keep inflation at around
3% but it can quickly get out of control,

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:36.030
especially when they just start producing
more money faster than origami rabbits.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.690
We call it hyper-inflation.

00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:43.220
The most extreme case of this was in Hungary
at the end of WW2.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:48.160
In 1944, the biggest note was 1000 pengő.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:55.480
By the middle of 1946 it was 100 quintillion,
that’s one followed by twenty zeroes.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:01.380
At its worst point, prices were doubling every
15 hours, so by the time you’d ordered a

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.980
goulash you already couldn’t afford it.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:09.830
The only solution was to completely replace
the currency and the Forint was introduced

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:11.000
in August 1946.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:20.340
At that point, the sum total of every single
pengo in the world, was worth just a thousandth

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.740
of a dollar.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:26.960
In the modern global world, you can have a
currency that is either floating or pegged.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.360
Floating currencies, such as the Pound, Euro
and Dollar, are worth whatever other countries

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:33.060
will pay for them.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:37.320
The governments do take steps to try and keep
things stable but they let the market dictate

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:38.660
their value.

00:05:38.660 --> 00:05:44.250
Pegged, or fixed currencies set a fixed rate
between their currency and the US Dollar,

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.090
or occasionally the Euro.

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:50.010
This is mainly to give it stability and if
a lot of their trade is with the US, like

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:55.530
Qatar and Jordan, or if they deal with mainly
US tourists, like in Barbados.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:59.910
They still have inflation and deflation like
everyone else but their government acts internally

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:01.520
to balance it out.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:07.300
During the cold war, Russia pegged its currency
to the US Dollar for obvious political reasons

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:12.430
but the rest of the world refused to accept
the ruble at this value so it was unusable

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:14.370
outside the Eastern Block.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:20.240
Not letting a small thing like money get in
the way, Pepsico worked with Moscow from 1972

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:25.889
by exchanging Pepsi syrup for the US distribution
rights to Stolichnaya vodka.

00:06:25.889 --> 00:06:30.230
When it comes to the world of the internet
and its blurry borders, it seems that even

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:35.080
a nation is not required for a currency to
get up and running, thanks to the rise of

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:39.950
crypto-currencies like Bitcoin, which first
appeared in January 2009.

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:45.520
It started out as basically valueless but
once the first purchase was made, two pizzas

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:51.230
in Florida, then the bitcoin began its rapid
journey to becoming a valid global currency,

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:55.760
with one bitcoin being worth over $1000 in
late 2013.

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:58.160
It’s not without its problems though.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:03.430
Although pitched as an anonymous currency,
that only applies if you are never associated

00:07:03.430 --> 00:07:05.380
with your bitcoin account.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:09.480
Because if you’re connected to your account,
the whole world will know you’ve been buying

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.990
steroids, pet monkeys and Make America Great
Again hats from China.

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:19.510
And like regular brick and mortar banks, there
are heists to deal with too.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:24.380
Just this August, a company called Bitfinex
was hacked for $72 million.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:30.680
As the Beatles once said, Money can’t buy
you love, but that was a long time ago now

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.470
and I’m pretty sure money has performed
pretty well against love since then, last

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:39.570
time I checked it was up 50 pips, which is
why all the bankers have a yacht in France

00:07:39.570 --> 00:08:06.139
and so many MPs make highly suspicious expense
claims two night 

00:08:06.139 --> 00:08:08.289
a week.

